(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with lung disease is being assessed for home oxygen. Her resting room-air saturation is 92%. Which is the next step in her qualification?","choices":["Order a complete blood count","Check her saturation with exertion","Stop: she does not qualify","Prescribe oxygen at 2 L/min now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 92% is above the 88% rest cutoff, so rest alone does not qualify her. Qualification is documented during rest, exertion and/or sleep, so the next step is to check her saturation with exertion (and sleep if indicated). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man on home oxygen for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease arrives with burns to his lips and nose after lighting a cigarette while wearing his cannula. Which hazard does this show?","choices":["Mucosal irritation","Vasoconstriction","Fire","Hypercapnia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A man on home oxygen lights a cigarette while wearing his cannula and burns his lips and nose. Oxygen is highly flammable, and fire is a listed hazard of oxygen therapy, which is why no smoking is taught for every home oxygen user. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a myocardial infarction and is placed on oxygen in the ambulance although his saturation was 98%. Which effect of oxygen makes this a problem?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Carbon dioxide retention","Mucosal irritation","Fire risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His saturation was 98%, so he was not hypoxic. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. Giving it to a man with a normal saturation exposes him to that effect without benefit. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman on home oxygen has dry, cracked lips and uses a petroleum jelly lip balm several times a day. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Use more balm to protect the lips","Switch her oxygen to a non-rebreather","Stop her home oxygen","Stop using petroleum-based products"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease are to be avoided with home oxygen. Her petroleum jelly lip balm, used several times a day, is exactly that, so she should stop it. Her dry lips are not a reason to keep using it. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man on home oxygen has a gas water heater with a pilot light in the hallway where he likes to sit. How far from it should his oxygen be kept?","choices":["At least 2 feet","At least 5 feet","At least 10 feet","At least 50 feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, which include pilot lights, electric appliances and candles. A gas water heater with a pilot light sits in the hallway where he sits, so his oxygen must be kept at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease, pulmonary hypertension and a hematocrit of 52% has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which finding makes her qualify?","choices":["Nothing; 89% does not qualify","Her pulmonary hypertension","Her hematocrit of 52%","Her interstitial lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is just above the Group I cutoff of 88%, so she needs a comorbidity. Pulmonary hypertension is one of the listed qualifying findings, so it makes her qualify as Group II. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a myocardial infarction. Her saturation is 93% on room air. A colleague wants to start oxygen by nasal cannula. Which is the right call?","choices":["Hold oxygen at a saturation of 93%","Start the nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Start a Venturi mask for her lung disease","Start a non-rebreather mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because of vasoconstriction. Her saturation of 93% is above that, so oxygen is held. Her chronic obstructive pulmonary disease does not change this, and a high fraction would add carbon dioxide retention risk. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a nasal cannula. Her breathing rate and depth change constantly, and her saturation swings with them. Which feature of a different device would give her a steady fraction?","choices":["A reservoir bag that holds oxygen","A higher flow on the same cannula","A mask that covers nose and mouth","A fixed fraction regardless of demand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A cannula gives a fraction that varies with respiratory rate, inspiratory flow and tidal volume, which explains why her saturation swings with her breathing. An air-entrainment device such as the Venturi mask gives a fixed fraction that does not fluctuate with patient demand. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with pneumonia is on a nasal cannula. His nurse turned the flow up to 8 L/min and his saturation is still 84%. He is alert and moving air well. Which is the next step?","choices":["Turn the cannula up to 12 L/min","Add a second nasal cannula","Lower the flow to 2 L/min","Change to a non-rebreather mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His cannula is already above its limit: the cannula covers 1-6 L/min and its fraction tops out at 44%, so more turning gains nothing. He is still at 84% but alert and moving air well, so he is ventilating well. The non-rebreather mask (60-100% at 10-15 L/min) is the step up for severe hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart disease and type 2 diabetes has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. He has no pulmonary hypertension, no cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease), and his hematocrit is 44%. Does he qualify for home oxygen at rest?","choices":["Yes, because his hematocrit is high","No, 89% needs a listed comorbidity","Yes, in Group I","Yes, in Group II because of diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% is above the 88% cutoff, so it needs a listed comorbidity: pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%. He has none, and his hematocrit is only 44%. Heart disease and diabetes are not on the list, so he does not qualify at rest. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman who smoked for 40 years has an arterial oxygen pressure of 57 mm Hg at rest on room air and a hematocrit of 58%. Which recertification interval applies to her home oxygen?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her arterial oxygen pressure of 57 mm Hg lies in the 56-59 range, above the Group I limit of 55 mm Hg, so she needs a comorbidity. Her hematocrit of 58% is above 56%, which is polycythemia. That puts her in Group II, which is recertified every 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on a nasal cannula at 6 L/min without humidification develops nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Which hazard of oxygen therapy is this?","choices":["Hypercapnia","Vasoconstriction","Oxygen toxicity of the lungs","Mucosal irritation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has been on 6 L/min through a nasal cannula without humidification, and now has nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Dry oxygen irritating the nasal lining is mucosal irritation, a listed hazard. Humidity is used when the flow is greater than 4 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a long-term tracheostomy after head and neck cancer needs humidified supplemental oxygen at home. Which interface delivers air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen to his airway?","choices":["Simple mask","Open face mask","Trach collar","Nasal cannula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He has a long-term tracheostomy and needs humidified oxygen delivered by an air-entrainment nebulizer. The trach collar is the air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over a tracheostomy, so it delivers to his airway directly. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and an echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which statement about home oxygen applies to her?","choices":["She qualifies in Group II","She qualifies in Group I","She does not qualify at 89%","She qualifies for sleep oxygen alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is above the Group I cutoff of 88% or less, so on its own it would not qualify. The echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension adds the comorbidity that makes a saturation of 89% qualify, which is Group II (recertified at 3 months). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after running a generator in a closed garage. He has a headache and is confused; pulse oximetry reads 98%. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen therapy","No oxygen, since saturation is normal","A nebulized bronchodilator","Breathing into a paper bag"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After exposure to generator exhaust in a closed garage, his headache and confusion fit carbon monoxide poisoning, which is a listed indication for oxygen therapy. The normal reading of 98% does not remove that indication, so oxygen is the treatment. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) has an arterial oxygen pressure of 58 mm Hg at rest. What is his qualification status?","choices":["Group I, recertified at 12 months","Not qualified at 58 mm Hg","Qualified for sleep oxygen","Group II, recertified at 3 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His arterial oxygen pressure of 58 mm Hg is above the Group I limit of 55 mm Hg but within the 56-59 range, which qualifies only with a comorbidity. He has cor pulmonale, so he is Group II, and Group II is recertified at 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with mild hypoxia, saturation 89%, had both nostrils packed for a severe nosebleed. She is comfortable otherwise. Which low flow device can still deliver her oxygen?","choices":["Trach collar","Non-rebreather at 15 L/min","Simple mask","Nasal cannula at 2 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both nostrils are packed, so a nasal cannula would not deliver. Her hypoxia is mild at 89%, and she is comfortable otherwise. A simple mask, a low flow device that covers the nose and mouth, can still deliver oxygen, at 5-10 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic lung disease has thick secretions she struggles to cough up during a severe exacerbation. Besides medication delivery, which listed rationale for a nebulizer applies?","choices":["Secretion clearance","Delivering a fixed oxygen fraction","Preventing carbon dioxide retention","Replacing her need for oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her thick secretions that she cannot cough up in a severe exacerbation match secretion clearance, which is a listed rationale for a nebulizer along with delivering liquid medication directly to the airways. The nebulizer helps move the mucus. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has attacks of severe one-sided headache around his eye, each lasting under an hour, several days in a row. His saturation is 99%. Which therapy is a listed special indication for him?","choices":["No oxygen, since he is not hypoxic","A bag-valve mask","Oxygen therapy","Nebulized saline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe one-sided headaches around the eye, brief and recurring daily for days, fit cluster headache, which is a listed special indication for oxygen. His saturation of 99% does not matter, because the indication here is the headache, not hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man's family turned his home nasal cannula up to 9 L/min because he felt short of breath. About what fraction of inspired oxygen does he receive at that setting?","choices":["44%","56%","60%","100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula can be turned to any setting, but the fraction stops at 44% (reached at 6 L/min). At 9 L/min he gets no more than that, so the extra 3 liters add oxygen to his nose but not to his fraction. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has a daytime saturation of 95%. Overnight oximetry shows her falling to 91% at the lowest. Does she qualify for nocturnal oxygen?","choices":["Yes, since she drops during sleep","Yes, in Group II","Yes, if she also has diabetes","No, her sleep value stays above 88%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep qualification needs a saturation of 88% or less with a drop of 5% or more from baseline. Her lowest value of 91% is above 88%, and 95 minus 91 is only a 4% drop. She fails both parts, so she does not qualify. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman in hospice with metastatic cancer has air hunger (a distressing sense of not getting enough breath). Her saturation is 95%. Which therapy is a listed special indication?","choices":["An endotracheal tube","Oxygen for symptom relief","No oxygen, since saturation is normal","A home oxygen qualification test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The hospice patient has air hunger, a distressing sense of not getting enough breath, and a saturation of 95%. Air hunger is a listed special indication, and symptom relief is a listed benefit of oxygen, so oxygen is given for comfort even without hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lung disease drops from 94% to 86% while walking. Oxygen at 2 and then 4 L/min is added, but her saturation stays at 86% while walking. How does this result bear on ambulatory oxygen?","choices":["It does not meet the exertion criterion","It qualifies her for ambulatory oxygen","It qualifies her in Group I at rest","It qualifies her for sleep oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation falls from 94% to 86% on walking, which is at or below 88%. But the exertion criterion also requires that the saturation improves when oxygen is given, and at 2 and 4 L/min it stays at 86%, so she does not meet the exertion criterion. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with severe pneumonia is on an aerosol mask from an air-entrainment nebulizer. The team wants to raise its fraction to 0.80. Why is that setting a concern?","choices":["It cannot go above 0.28","It is recommended above 0.50","It changes with his breathing rate","It is not recommended above 0.50"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A fixed-fraction air-entrainment nebulizer can deliver higher fractions, but setting above 0.50 is not recommended. His team wants 0.80, which is beyond that ceiling, so it is a concern even though his pneumonia is severe. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on home oxygen restores old cars in his garage and rubs grease off his hands with an oil-based cleanser while wearing his cannula. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Use a Venturi mask when working","Avoid petroleum-based oils and grease","Turn the flow up while working","Wear a simple mask in the garage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to avoid petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease. He is wearing his cannula while rubbing grease off with an oil-based cleanser, so he should avoid those petroleum oils and grease. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is on oxygen while his fluid overload is treated. Over two days his breathing eases, his saturation stays at target, and his vital signs settle. Which ongoing step is part of evaluating his oxygen therapy?","choices":["Start home oxygen qualification today","Keep monitoring for complications","Stop checking his saturation","Raise his flow to secure the gains"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evaluating response ends with vital signs and further monitoring for complications. His breathing has eased and his saturation holds, but he remains on oxygen, so the hazards of oxygen therapy still need watching. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a Venturi mask set at 28%. His saturation is still below his target, and he is comfortable and alert. Which adjustment raises his fraction precisely?","choices":["Add a reservoir bag to the mask","Switch to a nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Change to a higher-fraction port","Switch to a non-rebreather at 15 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A Venturi mask changes its fraction through different ports (24-50% when flow is adjusted from 4-10 L/min). He is stable and comfortable at 28% but below his target, so the precise adjustment is a higher-fraction port. It keeps the fraction fixed. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with interstitial lung disease has a resting room-air saturation of 93%. When he walks the hallway it falls to 85%, and with 2 L/min by nasal cannula it rises to 92% while walking. What does he qualify for?","choices":["Nothing, since rest is normal","Oxygen during sleep","Oxygen during exertion","Oxygen at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At rest his saturation of 93% is above 88%, but walking drops it to 85%, at or below 88%, and with 2 L/min by cannula it rises to 92%. The exertion criterion is a saturation of 88% or less that improves when oxygen is given, so he qualifies for oxygen during exertion. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man on postoperative day 1 after a knee replacement has an oxygen saturation of 89% on room air. He is comfortable, speaking full sentences, with no distress. Which device is the usual first choice?","choices":["Simple mask at 10 L/min","Venturi mask at 50%","Nasal cannula at a low flow","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After knee replacement he is comfortable and speaking full sentences with a saturation of 89%, which is mild hypoxia. The nasal cannula, a low flow device set at 1-6 L/min, is the usual first choice for mild hypoxia and gives 24-44%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is found unresponsive and is not breathing. He has a pulse. A colleague reaches for a non-rebreather mask. Which piece of equipment fits his situation instead?","choices":["Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula","Venturi mask","Bag-valve mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He is unresponsive and not breathing, so he is not ventilating on his own. A non-rebreather only improves the oxygen content of what a breathing patient inhales. The bag-valve mask, a resuscitator bag, supplies the breaths for a patient with a pulse who is not breathing. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
